# Same header as the [[headers]] block in the repo-root netlify.toml, and the
# same reason: keep every path out of search engines. Both exist because they
# cover different deploy paths, and only one of them reaches this site.
#
#   netlify.toml  — read on a repo-connected build. That is how anyone who
#                   clones this repository and points Netlify at it deploys, so
#                   the block has to be there.
#   this file     — travels INSIDE the publish directory, so it applies however
#                   the directory got there.
#
# MEASURED, not assumed: this site is deployed as a pre-built directory
# (`netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --no-build`) from a git WORKTREE, and the
# CLI resolves netlify.toml by walking up out of the worktree — it picked up a
# different branch's file, which carries no headers block, and the header was
# absent from the live response. A file in the publish directory has nothing to
# resolve.
#
# If they ever disagree, netlify.toml wins. Keep them saying the same thing.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
